    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d488">

                <pb facs="0598" n="340" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg340"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 300.115Ev2/17</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-03-02T19:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 2, 1915, 7 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received March 3, 4.00 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1728. Naval Attaché Gherardi returned from Bremen where I sent him to get
                    statements of captains of ships <hi rendition="#italic">Evelyn</hi> and <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Carib</hi>. Both boats sunk by mines, no false
                    directions given by British as reported here, boats simply ran on mine field.
                    Reported to me privately that Scandinavian countries will convoy their merchant
                    vessels using converted merchant vessels with naval crews.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
